    "client_msg_id": "2fa135ea-a97e-4282-8b44-c900d1774b1c",
    "type": "message",
    "user": "U08CR7HUWDC",
    "text": "\u003c@U0CA4S2GS4B\u003e\nNot yet. Until we close \u003chttps://github.com/datazip-inc/olake/issues/1251|github.com/datazip-inc/olake/issues/1251\u003e, you'll still need to pass `--state`. If you leave it out, OLake starts with empty state and re-syncs everything from scratch.\n\nIf you'd like to avoid the script for now, there's a workaround: S3 mode. You pass your file paths as `s3://...`, OLake reads them from S3 at the start of the run, and it writes `state.json` back to the same location after a successful sync. It's not a complete solution, though:\n• The state file has to exist in S3 before the first run. Start it with `{}`.\n• In S3 mode, logs go to stdout only. If you want to keep logs per sync, you'll need to capture the container output yourself (for example with `docker logs`).\nHere are the env vars for S3 mode: \u003chttps://olake.io/docs/install/olake-ui/#s3-log-file-storage-configuration:~:text=OLAKE_STORAGE_MODE%3A%20s3,your%2Dsecret%2Dkey|olake.io/docs/install/olake-ui#…\u003e",
